    "text": "To find \\(\\cos 135^\\circ\\), we can use the unit circle and the properties of trigonometric functions. The angle \\(135^\\circ\\) is in the second quadrant, where the cosine function is negative. We can express \\(135^\\circ\\) as \\(180^\\circ - 45^\\circ\\). Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\cos 135^\\circ = \\cos (180^\\circ - 45^\\circ)\n\\]\n\nUsing the cosine subtraction formula, we know that:\n\n\\[\n\\cos (180^\\circ - \\theta) = -\\cos \\theta\n\\]\n\nSo, substituting \\(\\theta = 45^\\circ\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\cos 135^\\circ = -\\cos 45^\\circ\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(\\cos 45^\\circ = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\). Therefore:\n\n\\[\n\\cos 135^\\circ = -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\cos 135^\\circ\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}\n\\]",
